WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.749
Narration production begins with writing.

2
00:00:02.749 --> 00:00:08.397
A good script is clear, conversational, and aligned with the screen or visual it supports.

3
00:00:08.397 --> 00:00:13.450
It should explain relationships, guide attention, and provide useful context.

4
00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:16.720
It should not simply read a dense slide word for word.

5
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:24.597
When narration and on-screen text duplicate each other too closely, the experience can feel redundant and mentally crowded.

6
00:00:24.597 --> 00:00:28.610
Recording voiceover requires a practical production mindset.

7
00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:33.663
Use a quiet space, consistent microphone placement, and a stable speaking pace.

8
00:00:33.663 --> 00:00:40.722
Record in manageable sections, label files clearly, and keep the script version connected to the audio version.

9
00:00:40.722 --> 00:00:47.633
Re-recording is sometimes necessary, but careful script review before recording prevents avoidable churn.

10
00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:55.733
Audio cleanup can include trimming, reducing background noise, evening out levels, and improving consistency across modules.

11
00:00:55.733 --> 00:01:03.387
Tools such as Audition or similar audio editors can support these tasks conceptually, but the goal is not overproduction.

12
00:01:03.387 --> 00:01:08.663
Learners usually need clean, understandable audio more than dramatic processing.

13
00:01:08.663 --> 00:01:12.230
Too much processing can sound unnatural or distracting.

14
00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:16.020
Captions and transcripts are essential production assets.

15
00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:25.086
Captions support learners who cannot hear the audio, learners in shared or noisy spaces, and learners who process information better with text.

16
00:01:25.086 --> 00:01:29.916
Transcripts help review, searchability, accessibility, and maintenance.

17
00:01:29.916 --> 00:01:36.381
Caption timing should be checked enough that the text supports the audio rather than competing with it.

18
00:01:36.381 --> 00:01:39.651
Keep narration assets organized and easy to revise.

19
00:01:39.651 --> 00:01:48.122
Use module-based file names, preserve approved scripts, track caption files, and avoid mixing source recordings with final exports.

20
00:01:48.122 --> 00:01:56.594
Course maintenance becomes far easier when the team can find the exact script, audio file, and caption file that correspond to a module.
